Yes. For a lot of feelings, the sensation arrives first and the mind backfills the explanation.

Something like:

\text{felt state} \rightarrow \text{attention bias} \rightarrow \text{selected facts} \rightarrow \text{story} \rightarrow \text{reinforced feeling}

Fear finds evidence of danger. Shame finds evidence of defect. Anger finds violations. Love finds significance. Grief finds absence. Hope finds openings. Suspicion finds inconsistencies. Confidence finds affordances.

The facts may all be real. The distortion can happen in which facts get recruited, how heavily they are weighted, and what causal story connects them.

That means a useful move is not “ignore the feeling” or “the story is false.” It is:

Hold the feeling fixed and generate multiple stories capable of producing it.

Then ask which facts discriminate among them.

So instead of:

“I feel threatened, therefore X is threatening me.”

you get:

“Threat is the observed internal state. Now generate candidate causes: actual danger, uncertainty, memory activation, social ambiguity, fatigue, status loss, expectation violation, etc. What evidence would separate them?”

Same with positive states. Feeling lucky can make the world suddenly appear saturated with opportunity because attention begins collecting confirming material. Sometimes that genuinely exposes possibilities that were always there.

So perhaps the primitive is:

Feeling → story completion.

And the defensive/offensive upgrade is:

Feeling → many possible stories → discriminating facts → revised feeling.

That keeps the signal without becoming captive to the first narrative that successfully explains it.